## What do you think separates a Senior Software Engineer role from a Software Engineer?

Scope of ownership and judgment, not years served or syntax known. A software engineer is given a problem and solves it well. A senior engineer decides which problem is worth solving, and which one to leave alone.

Three things in particular. First, judgment under ambiguity, knowing when to ship lean and carry a little debt on purpose, and when that debt will quietly hurt you later. Second, ownership past the merge, the tests, the docs, the on-call, the production reality, not just the pull request. Third, multiplying the people around you through review, design input, and leaving the next engineer's job easier rather than only finishing your own.

The common thread is accountability for outcomes, not just output. A senior engineer keeps the code tied back to what the business and the user actually needed, and is trusted to make that call without being told.
